{"parentUuid":"ae5d9eda-b389-4991-9490-fcd9e213f4fd","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01HJu1Hs18j6x12W1uCrxuBL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have enough to diagnose. Here's the chain.\n\n**Why `entire review --findings` shows nothing**\n\n`--findings` reads JSON manifests from `.git/entire-review/manifests/`. You have exactly **one** there — from a May 7 review that ran in `.worktrees/redact`, and the loader's per-worktree filter (`manifest.go:236`) hides it from the main worktree.\n\n**Why your fresh review (12:36–12:52 today) didn't write a new manifest**\n\n`writePostReviewManifest` (`cmd.go:637`) writes a manifest only if it can match each agent run to a session-state file with `Kind == \"agent_review\"`. The matcher in `manifest.go:104` discards sessions whose `Kind` is anything else — including null. The session JSONs for today's review (`8ad97f8d…` claude-code, `019e0872…` codex) were already cleaned up, but the surviving sessions on disk all show `\"kind\": null`, and the logs show **zero** \"adopted review env\" or \"adoption skipped\" entries during the 12:36–12:52 window. That means the lifecycle hook ran without `ENTIRE_REVIEW_SESSION=1` in its environment — the silent early-return at `lifecycle.go:1042–1043`:\n\n```go\nif os.Getenv(review.EnvSession) != \"1\" {\n    return  // no log\n}\n```\n\nSo the env-var handshake `entire review → claude -p → entire <hook>` is broken on your machine: env isn't reaching the hook subprocess. Three branches of `adoptReviewEnv` log on failure (agent mismatch / SHA mismatch / bad JSON); only the \"env not set\" branch is silent — and that's exactly what you hit. Likely cause is that `claude -p` (the argv `entire review` uses for claude-code, `reviewer.go:32`) is sanitizing or not propagating env to its hook child processes.\n\n**Net effect:** every run silently no-ops the manifest write. `--findings` finds nothing, `--fix` has nothing to resolve.\n\n**Quick way to confirm and unblock**\n\nRun a review with file-level diagnostics, then check if the env actually reached the hook:\n\n```fish\nENTIRE_LOG_LEVEL=debug entire review --agent codex\n# in another tab while it runs\ngrep -E \"adopted review|adoption skipped|review manifest\" .entire/logs/entire.log | tail\n```\n\nCodex is a useful first probe because its launcher (`agent/codex/reviewer.go:39`) and Claude's are structurally identical, so if codex tags the session and claude doesn't, Claude's `-p` mode is the env-stripping culprit. If neither tags the session, the bug is upstream of the agent fork (e.g., the spawn path itself isn't running the AgentReviewer with `cmd.Env` populated).\n\nEither way, the fix likely belongs in #1145's adoption path — adding a more useful \"EnvSession unset\" debug log would have made this self-diagnosable, and falling back to a checkpoint-id/process-id heuristic in `writePostReviewManifest` when `Kind` matching turns up empty would make the feature degrade gracefully instead of silently producing nothing.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- The env-var handshake design is elegant when it works: each spawned agent gets its own `ENTIRE_REVIEW_*` env, so multi-tenant correctness is \"by construction\" with no shared marker file (CLAUDE.md anti-features explicitly bans the older marker-file approach). But it has one fragility this codebase didn't account for: it assumes every agent CLI propagates env to its hook subprocesses unmodified. When a CLI does anything cute with env (whitelist filtering, scrubbing for cache-key stability, `-p` short-circuit paths), the entire feature silently no-ops.\n- The \"silent failure\" in `adoptReviewEnv` line 1042 is exactly the kind of pattern `superpowers:silent-failure-hunter` exists to catch — every other branch logs at WARN, but the most-common-in-practice failure mode logs nothing. That's why this issue is so opaque from the outside: there's no error, no warning, no