{"parentUuid":"b005c9da-3cd2-4dc2-b761-bf105eb99db4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01JjUGWpeWVHJaFVjBd9iF6v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three converging causes — none of them caught the bug because each one sits in a blind spot of the next.\n\n**1. The test pyramid never exercised real env propagation.**\n\nEvery test stubs out the chain at a different layer:\n\n- `reviewer_test.go:40-62` checks `cmd.Env` on the unexecuted `*exec.Cmd` — verifies the env was set, never runs anything.\n- `lifecycle_test.go:1298+` uses `t.Setenv(...)` to put the vars in the *test process's* env, then calls `adoptReviewEnv` directly — verifies the function reads env correctly, never goes through a subprocess.\n- `integration_test/review_test.go:41-50` builds a synthetic `reviewEnv` slice and hands it to `SimulateUserPromptSubmitWithReviewEnvVars` — verifies the adoption pipeline if the env is present, never tests that it gets there.\n- The Vogon canary (`e2e/vogon/`) doesn't cover the `--findings`/`--fix`/footer code path at all.\n- Real-agent E2E tests are gated behind `mise run test:e2e` and aren't part of `test:ci`, so they don't block merges.\n\nThe PR's verification block (`go test ./cmd/entire/cli/review ./cmd/entire/cli ./cmd/entire/cli/settings`) ran exactly these green-but-blind tests. So \"all tests pass\" gave full confidence in a propagation chain whose middle link was never executed.\n\n**2. The design assumed a property that doesn't always hold.**\n\n`AppendReviewEnv` (`env.go:94`) and `adoptReviewEnv` (`lifecycle.go:1037`) form a contract: parent sets env on agent process, agent inherits, agent's hook subprocess inherits transitively. That works *if* the agent CLI passes its own env to hook subprocesses verbatim. Claude Code in `-p` (one-shot) mode is the most likely violator — `claude -p` is the argv `entire review` uses for claude-code (`reviewer.go:32`), and `-p` mode has historically had different lifecycle semantics than interactive mode. Other launchable agents (codex, gemini-cli) plausibly behave the same way or differently; the design treated them all uniformly. The CLAUDE.md anti-features section even brags about ditching the older marker-file approach in favor of \"multi-tenant correctness by construction\" — that's correct *given* the propagation assumption, but the assumption itself was never validated.\n\n**3. The failure mode collapses three signals into one silent return.**\n\n`lifecycle.go:1042-1043`:\n\n```go\nif os.Getenv(review.EnvSession) != \"1\" {\n    return  // no log\n}\n```\n\nThis branch fires for two semantically different cases:\n- **Normal session** — silence is correct (don't spam logs for every non-review turn).\n- **Review session whose env was stripped** — silence hides the bug.\n\nThe hook can't distinguish them from inside, because the only signal that \"this *should* be a review\" is the env var that just went missing. So the bug presents identically to normal operation: no error, no warning, no missing-file message. Then three downstream features (manifest persistence, `--findings`, end-of-run footer) all gate on the same `Kind == \"agent_review\"` check at `manifest.go:104`, so a single silent miss kills them as a unit.\n\n**Net causal story:** PR #1145 replaced a marker-file design (which would have shown a stale or missing file diagnostically) with an env-var design (which fails invisibly when env is stripped); the test suite never executed the propagation chain end-to-end; the failure handler treats \"env missing\" as the no-op default; and three user-visible features were factored to share that single silently-failing check. Each of those choices is locally reasonable; together they produce a feature that ships green and silently no-ops in production.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- The deeper lesson is about **test coverage at the boundary you actually care about**. The PR has unit tests