{"parentUuid":"f049611b-1077-477b-901e-f2111393aa60","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01HaJxTmJRWR9wrG3WtYXYuh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four are real bugs, all separate from the current PR, all worth filing. Quick characterization of each:\n\n**1. Agent failure has no surfaced reason**\n\nWhen an agent process fails (non-zero exit, parser scanner.Err, RunError event), the user sees `✗ failed` in the dashboard and a final tally line — but the actual stderr/error message gets swallowed somewhere between the agent process and the user. Likely culprits: the parser (`parseClaudeOutput`/`parseCodexOutput`) emits `RunError{Err: ...}` but neither `TUISink` nor `DumpSink` may render the error text prominently in the failure case. Worth tracing one error event end-to-end through the sink chain.\n\n**2. Tokens column always empty**\n\nThe `TOKENS` column is fed by `AgentRun.Tokens`, which is populated by `TokensUpdate` events from each parser. If Claude's `-p` mode and Codex's stdout don't emit token counts in a format the parsers recognize (or if the parsers don't have `TokensUpdate` emission at all), the column stays empty for every run. Quick check: `grep TokensUpdate cmd/entire/cli/agent/*/reviewer.go` — if the result is empty or only emits zero tokens, that's the whole bug.\n\n**3. Ctrl+O drill-in is unhelpful**\n\nTwo distinct problems wrapped together:\n- During the run: drill-in shows event buffer but live output isn't tailed/scrollable\n- For long output: doesn't paginate or scroll, just truncates\n\n`tui_detail.go` is where this lives. Likely missing `viewport.Model` from bubbles for scrolling, and probably doesn't auto-scroll on append.\n\n**4. Codex reviewed against wrong branch (`issues...HEAD` instead of `main...HEAD`)**\n\nThis is the highest-impact one. Confirmed by reading `scope.go:122-205`: `detectScopeBaseRef` picks the merged-into-HEAD branch with the **most-recent tip timestamp** as the scope base. If you have any branch (`issues`, an old feature branch, a stale local) whose tip commit timestamp is newer than `main`'s, that branch wins — regardless of whether it's actually the branch you want to review against. The doc comment frames this as \"stacked PR support\" (prefer immediate parent over main), but in practice it picks unintended bases very easily.\n\nThe fallback chain (`origin/HEAD → origin/main → origin/master → main → master`) only fires when **no** ancestor branches exist, which is rare. So the smart-but-fragile timestamp heuristic dominates in practice.\n\nThis bug fires on **every review run with stale local branches in the repo**. It's wasting agent tokens reviewing the wrong scope, every time. Plausibly the highest-leverage bug of the four.\n\nThe fix is a design question, not a code question:\n- Option A: invert the priority — `origin/main` first, fall back to \"closest ancestor\" only when explicitly opted in.\n- Option B: add a `--base <ref>` flag so users can override.\n- Option C: detect the typical \"PR target\" via `gh pr view --json baseRefName` if `gh` is available; fall back to `origin/main`.\n- Option D: keep current heuristic but require the picked base to also be reachable from `origin/main` (i.e., must be an ancestor of mainline).\n\nEach has tradeoffs. Worth a brainstorm before any code.\n\n**Recommendation**\n\nFile these as four separate GitHub issues:\n\n| # | Issue | Severity | Fix size |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Agent failure errors not surfaced to user | High | Small (1–2 files, sink rendering) |\n| 2 | Tokens column always empty | Medium | Small if parser missing emit; medium if parser exists but agent output doesn't include tokens |\n| 3 | Ctrl+O drill-in lacks scrolling/live tail | Medium | Medium (viewport refactor in `tui_detail.go`) |\n| 4 | Scope detection picks wrong base branch | **High** | Small if Option A; medium if Option C |\n\nWant me to:\n\n1. **File the four issues as GitHub issues** with the diagnostic context above so they're tracked?\n2. **Dig into the codex-wrong-scope one** (#4) to confirm exactly which branch was picked and why — since \"this was just merged\" suggests there might have been a recent regression worth tracing?\n3. **Both** — file all four, then take #4 as the immediate next investigation since it's user-visible and behavioral?\n\nOr just leave them in this conversation as a record? Your call.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- The four bugs you're reporting share a common shape: each one *silently degrades* a feature that's documented to work better than it does. Failures don't crash, tokens don't error, drill-in doesn't say \"no scroll available,\" and scope detection silently picks wrong. The review feature has a systemic observability gap, not four unrelated bugs. That suggests the right framing for the issues is \"review observability sweep\" with four sub-tickets, rather than four independent bugs — there's value in addressing them as a coordinated pass with a shared design principle (\"review failures should always be diagnosable from the user's terminal output\").\n- Bug #4 (scope detection) is the kind of design choice where the original author was solving a real problem (stacked PRs) and the heuristic was correct for that case, but the heuristic's domain was much narrower than the real-world cases it now handles. The \"most-recent tip wins\" rule works perfectly when stacked PRs are the only non-main ancestor branches; it fails immediately when stale local branches exist, which describes most real developer machines. This is a pattern worth naming: heuristics tuned to the implementer's mental model break for users whose mental model differs. A `--base` flag (Option B) is often the right answer not because heuristics are bad, but because *users disagree about what the correct heuristic is* and the codebase shouldn't have to pick one.\n- The fact that you said \"I thought we fixed this\" about