{"parentUuid":"f4a74c5f-3037-4066-b3fc-f011ae07aae0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01QqTZTBw7Lo4WzVzKasf3Tg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That symptom is exactly explained by the same bug. Here's the chain:\n\nThe prompt that reaches codex is composed in `prompt.go:46`:\n```\nScope: review only the commits unique to this branch vs origin/soph/add-codex-post-tool.\n```\n\nWhen the base is `origin/soph/add-codex-post-tool`, codex faithfully reviews `origin/soph/add-codex-post-tool...HEAD`. That range is computed by git as \"commits in HEAD not in the base.\" Since `origin/soph/add-codex-post-tool` was merged into main *before* you branched, it's an ancestor of main but lots of mainline commits came after it. So `<base>...HEAD` includes:\n\n- Every commit that landed on main *after* `soph/add-codex-post-tool` was merged (PRs #1145, #1150, #1157, #1158, #1163, #1166, etc.)\n- *Plus* your actual branch commits\n\nThat's why the review \"had nothing to do with your branch\" — the scope was technically correct given the (wrong) base, but it dragged in 30+ commits of unrelated work, drowning your actual diff in noise. Codex wasn't ignoring the scope; it was reviewing exactly what we told it to, and what we told it to was wrong.\n\nApproach A fixes both symptoms with one change. When base = `origin/main`, the scope range `origin/main...HEAD` is exactly the commits unique to your branch — codex reviews only your work, no upstream noise. No separate codex-specific fix needed.\n\nYou'll see this concretely after the fix lands: with `--base origin/main` (the new default), codex's review prose will start with something like *\"I reviewed `origin/main...HEAD` — N commits\"* instead of *\"I reviewed `origin/soph/add-codex-post-tool...HEAD` — 31 commits\"*. Same agent, correct base, vastly different content.\n\nOK to proceed to writing the design doc with Approach A?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_seque